  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(1+2x)$ about $x=0.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n$x\\,\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n${x}^{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{\u2032}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{\u2033}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(0),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(1+2x) & \u27f9 &  & f(0) & =\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{2}{1+(1+2x)^{2}} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\frac{8(1+2x)}{(1+(1+2x)^{2})^{2}} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(0),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+x+\\frac{(-2)}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+1\\,x-1\\,{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
